# Project instructions

For this project, Codex must follow these rules:

1. Do not apply or change anything without the user's explicit consent.
2. Agree on any logic, plan, or approach with the user before implementation.
3. Thoughtfully and critically evaluate every proposal before presenting it.
4. Restate every user request as understood, then wait for the user's approval before applying changes.
4.1. Workflow approval shortcut: when the user explicitly invokes `$review-develop-push` and provides an explicit task list with wording such as `run for next tasks`, `review, develop, commit and push next tasks`, or equivalent, Codex must treat that message as explicit approval for the listed tasks only: mark them Approved, apply necessary task/docs updates found during review, implement them one by one, run checks, fix issues within scope, commit, push, and wait for CI. This shortcut does not apply if the user says review-only, no coding, do not commit, or if the task scope is ambiguous.
5. When the user asks for a review using phrases like "критически", "без фантазии", or "best practices", Codex must perform a full deep review on the first pass. The review must explicitly consider blockers, architecture, naming, boundaries, future-phase impact, tests/verification, no-goals, and consistency with project documents. For code/task deep reviews, Codex must inspect internal dependencies of the proposed changed or extracted functions/classes and external references from tests, scripts, routes, frontend, docs, and public API contracts before saying a task is ready. Codex must also check whether any proposed module location would create bad future dependencies for later approved extraction tasks, not only whether it works for the current task. Codex must not stop after the first issue it finds; it must complete the whole review pass, collect all material issues, and only then present the verdict or apply approved documentation/task updates. If Codex says "everything is good", "no questions", or recommends approval, it means Codex has already completed this deep review and is confident in that verdict.
6. For any deep review request, Codex must not give a final verdict until it has completed and briefly shown a review checklist. The checklist must include: exact scope being reviewed; internal dependency check for every proposed moved/changed function/class; external reference check across scripts, tests, routes, frontend, docs, and public API contracts; constants/config/defaults check; import direction/circular dependency check; future extraction dependency-direction check; backward compatibility check for existing callers and smoke scripts; verification/test plan check; batch update check confirming whether all found issues were handled together or explicitly left for later; and final verdict as ready, ready after listed changes, or not ready. If the user explicitly asks Codex to update a task/document after such a review, Codex must apply all approved docs-only/task fixes found in that complete pass together where practical, instead of iterating one issue at a time. If Codex later finds a new issue that should have been caught by this checklist, Codex must say plainly that the previous review was incomplete, then correct the task/instruction instead of treating it as a normal iteration. When the user asks "do you like this task?", "is everything good?", or similar after a deep review request, Codex must answer in this checklist format unless the full checklist was already shown in the immediately previous answer.
7. Codex must answer the user in Russian by default, including analysis, reviews, explanations, task discussion, status updates, and final answers. Use another language only when the user explicitly asks for it, or when the content is code, commands, identifiers, API fields, file names, exact technical labels, logs, or source text that should remain in English.
8. After any successful push made by Codex, Codex must wait for the GitHub Actions CI result and report either "CI passed" or "CI failed" with the failed workflow/job/step when available. Prefer `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\push_and_watch_ci.ps1` for push operations, or `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\watch_ci.ps1` immediately after a manual push.
8.1. Current project plan: the POC is closed after Phase 9.9 `Search Brief Value Replacement Update Resolver`. Phase 10+ persistence and later feature tracks are parked until the user explicitly reopens them. Any reopened work must preserve the Phase 9.8 role-anchoring guardrail, the human-approved runtime boundary, and the absolute product boundaries.

Absolute product boundaries:

9. The in-app AI agent must never perform direct web-search outside the approved backend search pipeline.
10. The product must never implement LinkedIn login.
11. The product must never scrape LinkedIn or bypass LinkedIn restrictions.
12. The product must never automatically send messages to candidates.
13. The product must never perform actions with user accounts or third-party accounts.
